  "query_preview": "..Shortwave ridging building into the Great Basin will\nbring large scale subsidence across the forecast area through the\nremainder of the day. Lingering show showers continue to wane late\nthis morning, and will end by early afternoon as this upstream\nridge continues to build east. As such, aLlowed the remaining \nwinter wx headlines to expire as scheduled. With temperatures. A\ncool and increasingly stable airmass settling in across the\nforecast area will keep daytime temperatures several degrees below\nnormal for the latter portion of November. Temperatures tonight\nwill trend a few degrees warmer across southern Utah, and will\nremain above freezing across lower Washington County, thus no\nadditional freeze products are anticipated.\n\nThe upstream shortwave ridge will move east on Sunday allowing\nsouthwesterly flow to overspread the forecast area in advance of\nan approaching shortwave trough. The resultant warming trend will\npush daytime temperatures back near climo Sunday. This\nprogressive, moisture starved trough quickly translate across the\nregion Monday, bringing a chance for mainly mountain showers with\nminimal accumulation. \n\nLongwave ridging is forecast to build across the region for the\nmiddle to latter portion of the week. A splitting shortwave trough\nfighting its way through this mean ridge position is forecast to \ntranslate through the Great Basin region late Wednesday into \nearly Thanksgiving Day. Given the splitting nature of this wave, \nforcing/moisture will remain limited, and like the early week \nsystem, the chance for precipitation is low (20-30%) and largely \nconfined to northern Utah.",
